  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on comparing the minimum probabilities of the lotteries and selecting the one with the higher minimum probability. In this case, Lottery B has a much higher minimum probability (100% vs 1%). The individual’s verbal report reflects a concern about risk, particularly the risk of gaining nothing for an extra 100€, which aligns with an aversion to low-probability negative outcomes. While the individual does not explicitly mention 'minimum probability,' the focus on the risk of ending up with nothing (the 1% event) and rejecting it in favor of certainty suggests implicit attention to the lowest-probability, worst-case outcome. This is consistent with the logic of the decision reason, which prioritizes the lottery with the higher minimum probability (i.e., less exposure to rare but bad outcomes). Although the reasoning is expressed in terms of risk-reward trade-off rather than a formal comparison of minimum probabilities, the underlying cognitive process appears aligned with the decision reason. The individual is effectively prioritizing security by avoiding the small chance of getting nothing, which is the core idea behind favoring higher minimum probability.",
  "final_assessment_score": 85
